<header><p class="enc-kicker">Work / monument · monument</p><h1 class="enc-title">Jokhang</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">7th century</span> · <a href="/centuries/7th-century">7th century</a> · Also known as Jokhang Temple</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Jokhang is the most sacred temple of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tibetan-buddhism">Tibetan Buddhism</a>, in the centre of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lhasa">Lhasa</a>, traditionally founded in the seventh century during the reign of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/songtsen-gampo">Songtsen Gampo</a>. It houses the Jowo, a statue of the Buddha said to have been brought by the Tang princess Wencheng, and pilgrims circle it on the Barkhor. The pillar bearing the treaty of 821–822 between <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tibet">Tibet</a> and the Tang stands before it.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/jokhang.jpg" alt="Jokhang" width="320" height="240" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: onwardtibet.org · CC BY-SA 2.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Jokhang_Temple_in_Tibet.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>The Jokhang stands in the centre of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lhasa">Lhasa</a>, in Barkhor Square, about 1,000 metres east of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/potala-palace">Potala Palace</a>. It was built under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/songtsen-gampo">Songtsen Gampo</a>, the first king of a unified <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tibet">Tibet</a>, who planned twelve temples across the country, with the Jokhang at its heart. After the king's death the city, once called Rasa, became Lhasa, place of the gods, and the temple took the name Jokhang, house of the Jowo, after its main image.</p><p>Tradition tells how the king tossed his hat and promised to build where it landed, in a lake where a white stupa then appeared. The lake was filled, leaving a small pond now seen as a well. In another version Queen <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bhrikuti">Bhrikuti</a>, the Nepalese wife of the king, founded the temple to hold the statue of Akshobhya Buddha she had brought. Craftsmen from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nepal">Nepal</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a> are credited with its design and construction.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">What it is</h2><p>The temple covers 2.51 hectares and faces west, towards <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nepal">Nepal</a>, in honour of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bhrikuti">Bhrikuti</a>. It is built of wood and stone in the Tibetan style, with influences from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a>, Nepal and Indian vihara design, and its roof is covered with gilded bronze tiles and topped with golden deer flanking a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dharma">Dharma</a> wheel.</p><p>The four-storey Buddha Hall holds the Jowo Shakyamuni, a gilded bronze statue 1.5 metres tall showing the Buddha at the age of twelve. It was brought to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tibet">Tibet</a> by the Tang princess Wencheng, kept first at the temple of Ramoche and later moved to the Jokhang, and it has become the most revered statue in Tibet. Outside stands a pillar 5.5 metres high with an inscription dated 821 or 822 recording a treaty between the Tibetan king Ralpacan and the Chinese emperor Muzong that set the frontier between their lands.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reception and legacy</h2><p>Pilgrims walk around the temple on the Barkhor, spin prayer wheels and prostrate themselves before the Jowo. The temple's history includes hard times. During the reign of Tresang Detsan, from 755 to 797, Buddhists were persecuted, the images were hidden or moved and the monks were driven out. The Fifth Dalai Lama carried out the last major renovation in 1610. In 1966 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/red-guards-china">Red Guards</a> attacked the temple, and for a decade there was no worship. Renovation began in 1972, and the temple was mostly complete by 1980. It became a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/unesco-world-heritage-site">UNESCO World Heritage Site</a> in 2000, as an extension of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/potala-palace">Potala Palace</a>.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Jokhang” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The peace treaty Tibet swore with Tang China in 821–822 was carved in Tibetan and Chinese on a stone pillar that still stands in front of the <b>Jokhang</b> temple in Lhasa.</blockquote><a href="/empire/tibetan-empire">Tibetan Empire · At a glance</a></li></ul></section>
